  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nA cold front extending roughly from Erie, PA southwest to Marion, OH \nhas become the focus for light rain showers developing this \nafternoon. A few rumbles of thunder may develop this afternoon as \nadditional daytime heating increases instability, however given the \noverall weak environment across the area, not expecting any of these \nto turn severe. Behind the aforementioned boundary, an expansive \ndiurnal cu field has developed, but given limited moisture not \nexpecting any showers. Conditions should dry out by tonight with the \nexception for portions of the snowbelt that may get isolated lake \nenhanced showers into Friday morning. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nHigh pressure is expected to begin to build east into the area on \nFriday, becoming the dominant weather pattern by Saturday. This high \nwill persist into early next week and should allow for a prolonged \nperiod of dry conditions across northern Ohio and northwest \nPennsylvania. In addition, this high will be responsible for \ntemperatures once again climbing to above average by late this \nweekend into early next week. Highs are expected to once again climb \ninto the upper 80s to low 90s with max heat indices in the mid 90s. \nOvernight lows will remain warm with temperatures only dropping into \nthe upper 60s to low 70s. A mid week system may impact the area, \nultimately allowing for temperatures to return to near normal by mid \nto late week, but given model variations the confidence in the \ntiming of this is fairly low.",
